WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.719
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Justin Gast, who's an associate professor

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:09.580
of policy and government at George Mason University
and also author of several books, including

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.480
the forthcoming book Majority-Minority Out
next Tuesday.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.170
A really great having you on.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.200
I appreciate your time.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.320
Speaker 5: Thanks, Dave.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.320
It's great to be with you.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:26.170
Speaker 1: So when we kind of study and talk
about reactionary thinking and ideology, which

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:34.110
is often fear based, it's hard not to stumble
across fear being generated with this idea

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:40.940
that people different than you are going to
take over and become the majority in your

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.640
area, country, region, whatever.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.100
And that's going to be bad and that will be
dangerous.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.210
And, you know, some sometimes.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:54.360
Tucker Carlson sometimes says dirtier and
poorer will be the outcome of that.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.760
So this idea of minority majority groups and
the changing demographics has been used for

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.540
a very long time to achieve political goals.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.890
Can you talk a little bit generally to get
us into this about the different types of

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:13.850
reactions that societies have when we see
the racial or ethnic balance shift?

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.880
Speaker 5: Sure, it's a great question, David.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.050
You know, it depends on whether we're talking
about this at the individual level or at the

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:27.640
societal level and at the societal level,
we know precious little about how big societies

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.750
governments respond to great demographic change,
and that's really what motivated this book

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:33.750
majority.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:39.550
Now, at the individual level, there is actually
extensive research what happens when we alert

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.860
people or simulate great demographic change?

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.960
And the key then and the findings are pretty
consistent.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:46.960
People are territorial.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.350
They're defensive, they're fearful, they're
insecure.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.580
And most of it is driven by uncertainty, by
the uncertainty of what will life be like

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.680
under these scenarios?

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:03.600
Speaker 1: And uncertainty generally comes
with the fear that it might not be so good,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:04.600
right?

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:09.600
I mean, uncertainty in a taken in a sort of
literal way could just mean we don't know.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.850
But oftentimes uncertainty is associated with,
well, that that sounds like it could be bad.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:18.190
Speaker 5: Well, it takes a special type of
human, I think, to sort of embrace uncertainty,

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:23.270
you know, and really, you know, human reactions
to uncertainty are going to be fearful because

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.840
of the insecurities associated with it.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.120
You fear what you don't know.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.260
You know, there are some people who sees the
future who sees the prospects of uncertainty.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.040
But yes, generally, you know, fear is is was
one of the primary emotions, if not just anxiety

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.040
or discomfort.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.150
Speaker 1: Are there societies that have been
able to kind of embrace demographic shifts

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.230
that are significant without significant conflict?

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.890
Speaker 5: So embrace is a strong word.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:54.200
I think that's actually a standard that I
don't think we even need to necessarily meet.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:03:00.170
I think what we really are looking for societies
that can foster and cultivate a culture of

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.599
coexistence.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:07.370
So my work is really concerned about whether
societies pivot towards conflict or towards

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.040
coexistence because there's always going to
be some degree of backlash, some degree of

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.040
discomfort.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.739
And I find that it to be universal in all
the different societies that I study that

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:20.450
have already undergone a majority minority
transition.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:25.500
And by that, I mean where the majority ethnic
or religious group loses its numerical advantage

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.239
to one or more minority groups.

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:33.770
And in those situations, you can always expect
some degree of conflict, some degree of anxiety.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:38.420
But the question is whether societies are
resilient and can redefine who they are in

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.720
order to evolve with the changing composition
of their people.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.510
Speaker 1: So can you give us some examples
of where it's gone on the better side and

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.510
on the not-so-good?

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.510
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:49.510
Sure.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:56.470
So I study six countries, six societies really
carefully in this book, and two societies

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.230
really pivoted towards suppression of minorities.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.019
The most overt kind of suppression takes place
in the Middle East region.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:07.380
And I study Bahrain, a small island off the
coast of the Arabian Peninsula, where you

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:12.220
effectively have a sort of second class system
for immigrants, but also a second class system

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:19.169
for people of a different religious sect than
that of the ruling Royal Court and party.

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:24.080
Alternatively, you can look at Singapore,
which is another society that is characterized

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.110
by suppression.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.889
But in a far more subtle way, Singapore is
concerned with evolving into a greater sort

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:31.210
of civic identity.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:36.460
But in its policymaking and the way it governs,
it actually reinforces ethnic divisions between

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:37.460
its people.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:43.039
Now, if you take a look at some American examples,
so Hawaii, which many people obviously associate

00:04:43.039 --> 00:04:49.319
as America's 50th state, Hawaii was previously
an independent kingdom and when it was an

00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:54.340
independent kingdom before it was forcibly
annexed by the United States in 1893, Hawaii

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:59.590
was actually a very multi ethnic and majority
minority monarchy.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:06.569
And in that society, Hawaii actually embraced
many of these newcomers and actually facilitated

00:05:06.569 --> 00:05:07.569
intermarriage among them.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:09.629
Now that's not to say there wasn't some nationalism.

00:05:09.629 --> 00:05:12.050
There wasn't some backlash there was.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:16.840
But there is a sort of greater understanding
of what it means to be a Native Hawaiian and

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.860
that it could be something that was shared
and bestowed upon someone that really took

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.400
on new meaning.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:26.849
In the 20th century after Americans took over
the archipelago.

00:05:26.849 --> 00:05:31.759
Because the Americans put in a very strong
assimilationist regime that basically treated

00:05:31.759 --> 00:05:38.229
any person of color, any person who was not
white Anglo American as someone to be dominated

00:05:38.229 --> 00:05:39.229
in a sense.

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:44.330
And so what you saw was a lot of intermingling
among Hawaiians with people who were previously

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:49.479
thought of as immigrants, the Japanese, the
Chinese, the Filipinos, and that intermixing

00:05:49.479 --> 00:05:52.969
create a preconception of what it means to
be Hawaiian in the future.

00:05:52.969 --> 00:05:54.729
Speaker 1: That's interesting.

00:05:54.729 --> 00:06:00.139
And there you know, it's very popular among
some in the United States to say Hawaii is

00:06:00.139 --> 00:06:03.789
just a mess and they give different reasons.

00:06:03.789 --> 00:06:10.229
Reasons why, but not often cited, is this.

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:13.900
I mean, it's interesting, you would think,
because Hawaii is a blue state, typically

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:14.900
votes.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:19.460
For Democrats, you would think that the right
might have wanted to seize on multiculturalism

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:24.630
is the reason why Hawaii is a mess economically
or whatever it is that that's often stated.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:28.219
But maybe it's not even necessarily that well-known
a story in the United States?

00:06:28.219 --> 00:06:29.219
I don't know.

00:06:29.219 --> 00:06:31.309
Speaker 5: Yeah, I don't think of Hawaii as
a mess at all.

00:06:31.309 --> 00:06:33.729
I think of it actually as a paragon in many
ways.

00:06:33.729 --> 00:06:38.039
You know, the way that they have been able
to embrace demographic change over the course

00:06:38.039 --> 00:06:43.860
of their history to broaden the understanding
of what it means to be Kanaka, to be a Native

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.749
Hawaiian is really actually exemplary for
how I think we want to evolve the civic identity

00:06:49.749 --> 00:06:51.649
of what it means to be American.

00:06:51.649 --> 00:06:57.669
Speaker 1: Very generally speaking, top level
there is the idea that in the United States,

00:06:57.669 --> 00:07:03.340
the way the political divisions are, the left
tends to be more accepting of demographic

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:10.620
changes, while the right tends to be more
fearful and critical of demographic changes.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:16.680
Can you flesh that out a little bit more in
terms of whether that general assessment is

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.840
always or mostly correct and maybe some of
the exceptions or kind of strange interplays

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:22.840
to this perspective?

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:29.039
Speaker 5: Sure, it wasn't always true, particularly
when the left was associated with unionism,

00:07:29.039 --> 00:07:33.810
which in historically speaking, you know,
we're talking 80 to 100 years ago was actually

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:40.099
relatively close and kind of reproduced a
lot of the social and cultural inequities

00:07:40.099 --> 00:07:41.770
in American history.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:45.120
But certainly in the contemporary moment,
that's true.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.550
Generally speaking, the left not only embraces
demographic change, it almost anticipates

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:50.550
it.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:55.089
You know, with the election of Barack Obama,
many Democrats crowed about demography being

00:07:55.089 --> 00:08:00.270
destiny and how this was the path to electoral
victory forever more after.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:06.800
And certainly, Republicans have not just,
you know, I think, you know, fear demographic

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.979
change, but they've actually leaned into that
fear and allowed it to consume much of its

00:08:11.979 --> 00:08:12.979
politics.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:18.279
Until now, in many ways, the Republican Party
is very nostalgic party right now, yearning

00:08:18.279 --> 00:08:23.530
for for an earlier period in American history
that when they can make America great again.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:27.719
And so, you know, in light of that demographic,
you know, the relationship with demographics,

00:08:27.719 --> 00:08:29.909
I think that is generally the case.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:34.850
But it's also been reinforced by our politics
ever since the Democratic Party really moved

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:38.790
to the center economically and embraced kind
of greater neo liberalism.

00:08:38.790 --> 00:08:42.470
The real differences between the Democratic
Party and the Republican Party in the United

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:46.650
States are principally on cultural issues
right now, right?

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:52.510
I mean, certainly there are economic differences,
but the real gaps are on cultural matters.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.871
And a lot of that is about how you treat minority
groups, whether they're minorities, because

00:08:56.871 --> 00:09:03.210
of their sexuality, because of their ethnicity,
because of their religion, whatever it is.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:07.020
You know, generally speaking, that is where
you see the greatest kind of rift.

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:15.030
Speaker 1: Yeah, it seems even things like
wanting a citizenship question on the census

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:20.190
or the focus on critical race theory to the
extent that any of the people focusing on

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:22.640
it understand what it is.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.180
These things seem to be not so indirectly
related to exactly these same issues we're

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.180
talking about.

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:30.570
Speaker 5: Oh, I think you're being modest.

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.380
They are directly related.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.960
OK, I'll take the demographic change.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:35.960
Absolutely.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:42.160
You know, one of the greatest dividers, but
also uniters in the in the course of history

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:47.360
of great demographic change in majority minority
transitions is the socialization of youth.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.930
You know how we actually educate our young
people going forward and our schools, the

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:57.490
sort of engines of of of integration and upward
mobility together?

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:01.370
How are you integrating your schools and are
you learning a common language and moving

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:02.490
forward together?

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.480
Or are you going to have debates over what
we teach in schools?

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:10.560
And many ways, you know, textbooks have have
actually have a long history of being debated,

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.550
whether they recognize the contributions of
minorities over history, how you treat historic

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:19.660
inequities and perhaps the inconvenient truths
of the country's history.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:20.830
That's quite common.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:25.310
And then, you know, with regard to census
questioning, that's a matter of power when

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.970
you are excluding, you know, people from the
population count.

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:31.160
You're also excluding them from power.

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:36.510
And so these questions are integral, and they're
produced by the fraught politics of demographic

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:37.510
change.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:41.710
Speaker 1: Can you give some other examples
of the sort of battles that are being had?

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.160
So we've got the citizenship question, critical
race theory.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.890
You've talked about textbooks and how history
is taught.

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:52.430
There's Let's make English officially the
one official language of the United States.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:57.370
What are some of the other kind of ways in
which we can see this battle thought?

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:03.120
Speaker 5: Sure, you can see it in the battles
over culture, over music and over art and

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.050
over sports.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:10.840
I think back to Colin Kaepernick's effective
cancelation from the NFL when he was taking

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:14.490
a knee before the national anthem in protest
of police brutality and.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:18.590
Discrimination, I think, of the battles over
Confederate monuments and and the desire to

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:19.740
protect them.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:20.740
Right.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:24.910
You can also look at the world of commerce,
know do we see racialized poverty?

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:29.610
Or do we see the marketplace as a as a place
of interdependency?

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:35.320
The politics of affirmative action and whether
it's education or employment, all of these

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:40.160
are very much related to demographic change
and how we manage it.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:45.870
Speaker 1: So there is the question there's
sort of empirical questions about is the demographic

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:51.710
change happening and what data points do we
have as you researched in the book about other

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.790
places that that it's happened, that's one
side of it.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:12:00.800
There's also the reality that for a lot of
people, this rhetoric really resonates, and

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.780
some at least have Donald Trump's rise to
power was, you know, Mexico's not sending

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:08.930
its best, China's treating us very unfairly,
etc.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:14.930
You know, Muslim terrorists are coming up
through the US-Mexico border.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:19.660
Ms-13 is being released into the community.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:23.660
All of this different stuff, it seems very
politically effective.

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:29.410
And I'm curious your thoughts on how can it
be diffused so that it's not so politically

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:30.410
effective?

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:32.230
Or is there something visceral about it that
just works?

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:36.370
Speaker 5: Sure, there is something visceral,
but it can be defeated.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:41.380
And, you know, politicians and public officials
more broadly, including even business leaders

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:45.810
and civil society leaders, have to become
more adept at calling out fear mongering for

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.410
what it does, it divides.

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:52.220
And if we're going to survive as a nation,
if we are and this is the most nationalist

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:56.810
thing I think I can say is you want to survive
as a nation and if you want to survive as

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:01.410
a nation, you have to cultivate some degree
of solidarity and unity.

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:06.860
And when you have, you know, politicians trying
to profit in the short run to win elections

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:12.560
with divisive rhetoric that doesn't have a
huge evidentiary basis, we're all going to

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:13.560
lose.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:16.420
And so I think that, you know, the opponents
of this kind of fear mongering have to call

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:17.510
those things out.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:21.850
You know, people like Donald Trump are trying
to divide American society, which weakens

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:26.670
us in the future and weakens us economically,
weakens us socially.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:28.830
And so I do think that that's the way forward.

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:33.520
But complementing that idea is you can't just
tear down the opposite side tactics.

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:38.250
You also have to have a strategic vision yourself,
and that's where the creativity comes in.

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:43.400
We have to actually cultivate an understanding
of who we are as a country in one in a way

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.940
that evolves from the historic understanding
of a white mainstream.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:52.870
It has to be one that is not necessarily hitched
to race or ethnicity or religion, but one

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:56.110
that's is about a civic sense of belonging,
a civic sense of purpose.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:57.480
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, it's tough.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:05.090
I guess we would benefit from having sort
of like a some kind of a PR consultant help

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:06.720
with this conversation.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:13.610
But you know, when they say immigrants commit
more crime and they're taking our jobs and

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:18.290
so on, I mean, it's very it's easy in a sense
to say that stuff's not true.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:22.680
And here we have all the studies and native
born Americans commit crime at a higher rate.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:23.740
And why wouldn't they?

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:27.400
They don't have a fear that they're committing
crime in a different country.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:32.100
You know, we can do all that stuff, but it
just seems like you're kind of, you know,

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:37.340
pushing a boulder up a mountain in a sense,
because it's just it's always tougher to to

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:40.420
interject facts when the emotions work so
well.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:41.420
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:45.220
Well, look, we have to invalidate these falsehoods,
you know, when they're being made.

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:47.810
We can't let them stand because they shouldn't
stand.

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:49.810
You know, they're completely false.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:52.170
And in some cases, they're just exaggerations.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:55.250
In other cases, they're absolutely flat false.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:56.420
But that's a negative strategy.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:59.279
You have to have an affirmative strategy to
right.

00:14:59.279 --> 00:15:03.351
And you know, whether it's a PR agency or
just some visionary, you have to work as a

00:15:03.351 --> 00:15:04.351
as a country.

00:15:04.351 --> 00:15:10.350
I think as a people with a million leaders,
not just one who is thinking clearly and strategically

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:13.720
about what holds us together as a country,
what holds us together as a nation

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:20.060
Speaker 1: and what is the best argument is,
is it basically this country is built on people

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:24.590
from all over coming here and contributing
what they have to contribute.

00:15:24.590 --> 00:15:28.100
And almost all of us are here for that reason.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:29.100
And it's it is.

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.360
